Hi Igor,

> One honest gap: I could not yet take the voltage-vs-frequency
> curve [...] Future work.

I took one on RK3576 this week, from the correctness side. Same wall
on the rail as yours, so it is one device tree per point.

Mainline sets no rate for CLK_RKNN_DSU0 and no board sets vdd_npu_s0,
so my ROCK 4D runs the NPU at 786.432 MHz on 750 mV, while Rockchip's
own table for this NPU asks 800 mV of its 800 MHz step at the worst
leakage bin. One core is exact there. Two cores with jobs in flight at
the same time are not: the second one writes single words wrong, and
the wrong value is the right value plus a bit of the accumulator. A
pass below is 5400 rows of a batched matrix multiply, each row against
the same multiply done one row at a time:

  786 MHz, 750 mV    11 to 25 wrong rows a pass
  594 MHz, 750 mV    0, 0, 0, 0
  786 MHz, 800 mV    0, 0, 0, 0
  786 MHz, 850 mV    0, 0, 0, 0

50 mV at one rate is the whole difference between exact and wrong, and
that is my answer to the OPP question you put to Tomeu: the column
that must not be dropped is the voltage one.

The part worth checking on RK3588 is that this is invisible to a
single threaded harness. Your rows are one inference thread with a
bit-exact oracle, and the fault here needs two cores busy at once. If
900 and 1000 MHz were taken with one NPU core busy, three of them at
850 mV are untested.

Happy to send the rest, how the bit was pinned down and why an
assigned-clock on the SCMI clock hangs this board, if it is useful.

Cheers,
